Root cause (scenario lesson):
Pure OPSEC failure, no exploit. One reused handle across three platforms collapses a pseudonymous influencer into a named person, and operational details (a covert campaign and its codename) were posted in a readable forum thread. 
The data was public by design; correlation did the rest.

Defensive takeaways:
Don't reuse handles across platforms. A shared username is the pivot that links otherwise separate profiles.
Keep operational detail off public channels. The campaign codename and target leaked because they were discussed in an open forum thread.
Lock down profile exposure. Hide connections, followers, and education where the platform allows, and run periodic self-OSINT to see what correlates.
Platform side: rate-limit and CAPTCHA profile enumeration, and flag coordinated account-creation bursts and mass-follow patterns.

Key lessons:
Start from the most structured identity source, then pivot outward on shared data points.
Small fragments (a name, a date, a codename) become strong intelligence once correlated across sources.
Account-creation timestamps are a reliable tell for coordinated/bot activity.

